## A R T I C L E I N F O
Keywords:
Benzene  Hematopoiesis  Niche  ROS  Toll/NF- \(\kappa\mathbf{B}\)  Drosophila
## A B S T R A C T
Hematopoietic stem cells/progenitor cells (HSC/HPCs) orchestrate the hematopoietic process, effectively regulated by the hematopoietic niche under normal and stressed conditions. The hematopoietic niche provides various soluble factors which influence the differentiation and self-renewal of HSC/HSPs. Unceasing differentiation/proliferation/high metabolic activity of HSC/HPCs makes them susceptible to damage by environmental toxicants like benzene. Oxidative stress, epigenetic modifications, and DNA damage in the HSC/HPCs are the key factors of benzene-induced hematopoietic injury. However, the role of the hematopoietic niche in benzene-induced hematopoietic injury/response is still void. Therefore, the current study aims to unravel the role of the hematopoietic niche in benzene-induced hematotoxicity using a genetically tractable model, Drosophila melanogaster. The lymph gland is a dedicated hematopoietic organ in Drosophila larvae. A group of 30–45 cells called the posterior signaling center (PSC) in the lymph gland acts as a niche that regulates Drosophila HSC/HPCs maintenance. Benzene exposure to Drosophila larvae (48 h) resulted in aberrant hemocyte production, especially hyper-differentiation of lamellocytes followed by premature lymph gland dispersal and reduced adult emergence upon developmental exposure. Subsequent genetic experiments revealed that benzene-induced lamellocyte production and premature lymph gland dispersal were PSC mediated. The genetic experiments further showed that benzene generates Dual oxidase (Duox)-dependent Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) in the PSC, activating Toll/NF- \(\kappa\mathbf{B}\) signaling, which is essential for the aberrant hemocyte production, lymph gland dispersal, and larval survival. Together, the study establishes a functional perspective of the hematopoietic niche in a benzene-induced hematopoietic emergency in a genetic model, Drosophila, which might be relevant to higher organisms.
## 1. Introduction
benzene is toxic even at low concentrations, World Health Organization (WHO, 2019) has set no safety levels for benzene exposure. Although benzene use has been controlled drastically, its level in the air has reached alarming levels in both developed and developing countries [8]. International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) has classified benzene as a group 1 carcinogen [9].
Several reports detailed the key contributions of the Toll signaling pathway in mounting immune response against several pathogens and stress conditions [18–20]. Toll signaling has been activated in the HSC/HPCs and hematopoietic niche, which participates in the hematopoietic process under normal and stressful conditions [21]. The role of NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\), a downstream transcription factor of Toll signaling, in HSC/HPCs differentiation has been observed in p65 knockout mice by Stein and Baldwin [22]. Benzene metabolites have also been reported to activate \(\mathbf{NF-KB}\) in bone marrow progenitor cells and erythroblasts, indicating a possible role of NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\) in benzene-induced hematopoietic malignancies [23,24]. Therefore, the current study aimed to decipher the potential role of Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\) signaling in the hematopoietic niche during benzene-induced hematopoiesis emergency in a genetically tractable animal model, Drosophila melanogaster.
Drosophila hematopoiesis occurs at the embryonic and larval stages, recognized as primitive and definitive hematopoiesis, respectively. In addition, dedicated hematopoietic hubs have also been identified in adult Drosophila, which can produce new hemocytes to counter immune challenges [25]. The lymph gland is a dedicated and well-characterized larval hematopoietic organ. Ontogenetically, the lymph gland (Fig. 1a) exhibits a clear ultra-structural distinction between a pool of progenitors located within the inner core region on the lymph gland termed the medullary zone (M.Z.), a differentiation zone (cortical zone, C.Z.) that contains differentiated hemocytes [2,26]. In addition, a narrow band referred to as the intermediate zone (I.Z.) between M.Z. and C.Z. has been characterized by intermediate progenitors (transition of hemocyte maturation) [27]. The lymph gland produces three types of hemocytes which are functionally akin to mammalian myeloid cells. These include 1. plasmacyttes (typically, macrophages help in phagocytosis), 2. crystal cells (like platelets, help in melanization), and 3. lamellocytes (the large flat cells that help encapsulate pathogens inside the host) [28]. Recently two single-cell RNA-Seq studies have demonstrated the heterogeneity and the developmental route of hemocytes. The first study by Cho et al., [29] characterized two new cell clusters, GST-rich (enriched with glutathione-S-transferase transcripts) and adipohemocyte (high expression of genes related to phagocytosis receptors, lipid metabolism, and starvation). While the other study by Girard et al., [30] identified clear intermediated progenitors and demonstrated the role of metabolism in the development of the lymph gland hemocytes. Along with M.Z. and C.Z., a small cluster of non-hematopoietic cells (30–40) localized posteriorly in the lymph gland called the Posterior Signaling Centre (PSC), is functionally parallel to the vertebrate hematopoietic
niche. Apart from PSC, studies demonstrated a functional role of the cardiac tube as a vascular niche in HPCs maintenance through Slit and Branchless (FGF-like receptors) [31,32]. Moreover, due to limited access to the adult mammalian hematopoietic niche in the bone marrow, Drosophila provides a unique experimental model to investigate the hematopoietic niche-dependent regulation of environmental chemical-induced hematopoietic injury. The organism is also approved by the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) and the European Centre for the Validation of Alternative Methods (ECVAM) [33].
This study demonstrates the functional role of PCS against benzene-induced hematopoietic injury. Benzene exposure elicited ROS in the PSC, subsequently activating Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\) in the lymph gland, which is essential to execute a proper immune response and might be helpful for larval survival upon benzene exposure.
