## 3. Results
## 3.1. Benzene exposure interrupts larval hematopoiesis in the lymph gland
Benzene, one of the hazardous chemicals, is known for its capacity to cause myelodysplastic syndromes [41,42]. Fig. 1a represents a schematic diagram of the Drosophila lymph gland (hematopoietic organ). To examine whether the benzene exposure affects the HSC/HPCs and hemocytes (plasmatocyte, crystal cells, and lamellocyte) numbers, the early 3rd instar larvae (72 h after egg laying) were exposed to three different concentrations of benzene (1.0, 10.0, and \(100.0\;\mathrm{mM})\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . The resulting \(\upbeta\) -integrin staining showed a concentration-dependent increase in lamellocyte differentiation in the lymph gland (Fig. 1b–f) in \(10.0\,\mathrm{\mM}\) \((\sim\!20\%\) , \(P\!<\!0.05)\) and \(100.0\,\mathrm{\mM}\) \((\sim\!75\%\) , \(P\!<\!0.001]\) ) of benzene exposed larvae as compared to control. However, no lamellocyte differentiation was observed in the lowest concentration, \(1.0\;\mathrm{mM}\) \((P\!>\!0.05)\) . Immunostaining of Hnt, an early crystal cell marker, revealed no significant differences in the crystal cell number at all benzene concentrations compared to control (Fig. 1g–k). The effect of benzene on plasmatocytes was determined with \(H m l{>}G F P\) by calculating the percentage area covered by GFP in the primary lobe of the lymph gland, as shown in Fig. 1l-p, the results revealed a marginally significant reduction in plasmatocytes in \(100.0\;\mathrm{mM}\) benzene \((P\!<\!0.05)\) as compared to the control.
The effect of benzene exposure on HSC/HPCs was examined using the domeMESO-GFP strain. We verified a decline of \(\sim\!15\%\) in the GFP area (correspond to HSC/HPCs) only in the highest concentration, 100.0 mM benzene \((P\!<\!0.005)\) , as compared to control (Fig. S2a-e). To examine whether the gradual decline in plasmatocytes and hematopoietic progenitors upon benzene exposure is due to cell death, we immunostained the lymph gland with anti-caspase3 antibody and observed a high level of cleaved caspase3 staining in medullary and cortical zones of the lymph gland upon \(48~\mathrm{h}\) of benzene exposure, however, no cell death was observed in the PSC (Fig. S2f-k). The above results suggest that benzene exposure adversely affects lymph gland hematopoiesis.
## 3.2. Prolonged benzene exposure leads to early lymph gland dispersal
In general, the lymph gland disperses during metamorphosis and releases the HSC/HPCs and differentiated hemocytes in circulation. However, the lymph gland can be dispersed prematurely due to hyperdifferentiation of lamellocytes during the immune challenge condition [43]. As we observed a massive lamellocyte differentiation, we asked whether the prolonged benzene exposure could lead to premature lymph gland dispersal. Therefore, first instar larvae were exposed to benzene for \(96\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , and the lymph gland dispersal was examined. The result revealed that prolonged benzene exposure led to a concentration-dependent increase in lymph gland disruption (Fig. 2a–e). Benzene exposure of \(10.0\ \mathrm{mM}\) and \(100.0\ \mathrm{mM}\) showed \(15\%\) \((P\!<\!0.001)\) and \(35\%\) \((P\!<\!0.001)\) lymph gland disruptions, respectively, as compared to control. Lamellocyte production and lymph gland dispersal are the prominent markers of active immune response in Drosophila. Premature lymph gland dispersal suggests the release of mature hemocytes produced in circulation to counter stressful conditions. Therefore, the data indicate that lamellocyte differentiation and premature lymph gland dispersal respond to benzene-induced immune challenges.
