Interestingly, Dif and Myd88 knockdown in the PSC showed a significant rescue \( _{\sim80\%} \) and \( 92\% \), respectively, \( P\!<\!0.001) \) in lamellocyte production after \( 48\ \mathrm{h} \) of benzene \( \left(100.0\;\mathrm{mM}\right) \) exposure compared to its genetic control, \( P c o l\,>\,G F P \) (Fig. 4d–j). Concomitantly, a significant reduction of \( {\sim}63\% \) and \( {\sim}75\% \) in the lymph gland dispersal was observed after \( 96\,\mathrm{h} \) of \( 100.0\;\mathrm{mM} \) benzene \( (P\!<\!0.001) \) exposure upon Dif and Myd88 knockdown, respectively, in the PSC as compared to its genetic control (Fig. 4k-q). Further, Toll overexpression in the PSC \( (P c o l{>}G F P{>}T o l l I O B) \) resulted in the dispersal of \( {\sim}60\% \) \( (P\!<\!0.001) \) of the lymph gland upon \( 100.0\,\mathrm{mM} \) benzene exposure of \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \) \( (P\!<\!0.001) \) as compared to their genetic control, \( P c o l{>}G F P \) (Fig. S3a-e). Furthermore, the role of the Toll/NF- \( \mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB} \) pathway in benzene-induced lamellocyte differentiation and premature lymph gland bursting was confirmed using another independent PSC-specific driver, \( A n t p{>}G F P \), results of which were in accordance with data obtained using \( P c o l{>}G F P \) (Fig. S3f-o). Altogether, these data suggest that the PSC mediates the activation of Toll/NF- \( \mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB} \) signaling and is decisive in mounting response against benzene-induced hematopoietic challenge.
3.5. Oxidative stress in the PSC mediates lamellocyte differentiation and lymph gland dispersion upon benzene exposure
ROS is a crucial factor in maintaining blood cell homeostasis. However, excessive accumulation of ROS results in oxidative stress leading to bone marrow dysfunction [45]. Previously, Louradour et al., [43] have established that elevated ROS levels in the PSC upon wasp infestation led to aberrant lamellocyte production and premature lymph gland disruption. To understand the role of ROS in the benzene-induced hematopoietic challenge, first, we monitored the ROS levels using a lacZ reporter line under the control of Glutathione S-transferase D promoter gstD-lacZ. In agreement, the lymph gland of unexposed larvae showed negligible ROS in the PSC and moderate ROS levels in the MZ, whereas significant ROS accumulation was observed in both the PSC as well as in the lobes of the lymph gland upon \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \) benzene exposure (Fig. 5a and b). Quantification of lacZ intensity showed a significantly high level of ROS
in the PSC in \( 10.0\,\mathrm{\mM} \) ( \( \sim\!2 \) -fold, \( P\!<\!0.05) \) and \( 100.0\,\mathrm{\mM} \) ( \( \sim\!7 \) -fold, \( P\!<\!0.001) \) as compared to control (Fig. 5c). The lymph gland exposed to the lowest concentration \( (1.0\ \mathrm{mM}) \) did not show elevated ROS levels (Fig. 5c). Duox (Dual oxidase) is a transmembrane NADPH oxidases mediates the cellular ROS generation [46]. To examine the involvement of Duox in benzene-induced ROS generation, Duox was knockdown in the PSC \( (P c o l{>}G F P{>}D u o x\ R N A i) \), and the ROS generation was analyzed through DHE staining. Targeting Duox in the PSC led to a significant reduction ( \( 100\% \) rescue, \( P{<}0.001) \) of ROS levels in the PSC and the lymph gland (Fig. 5d–g, j). A similar result was obtained when immune-related catalase (IRC, extracellular catalase) was overexpressed in the PSC (Fig. 5 h-i, j).
Furthermore, \( P c o l{>}G F P{>}D u o x\ R N A i \) lymph glands showed a reduction in lamellocyte differentiation \( (\sim\!75\%,P\!<\!0.001) \) at \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \) and lymph gland dispersal \( (\sim\!90\%,P{<}0.001) \) at \( 96\,\mathrm{h} \) of benzene exposure. A similar trend \( [(\sim\!70\% \) reduction in lamellocyte differentiation \( (P{<}0.001) \) and \( \sim\!75\% \) reduction in lymph gland dispersal \( (P{<}0.001) \) was observed upon overexpression of extracellular catalase \( (P c o l{>}G F P{>}I R C) \) in the PSC (Fig. 5k–x). Benzene is known to produce intracellular ROS [47], therefore, we overexpressed an antioxidant enzyme superoxide dismutase (SOD2) in the PSC \( (P c o l{>}G F P{>}S O D2) \) and monitored the lamellocyte production and the lymph gland dispersal. SOD overexpression in the PSC resulted in the significant reduction of the lamellocyte output in \( 10.0\,\mathrm{mM} \) ( \( 100\% \), \( P\!<\!0.001) \) and \( 100.0\,\mathrm{mM} \) \( (\sim\!50\%,P\!<\!0.001) \) groups as compared to their respective genetic control, \( P c o l{>}G F P \) (Fig. S4a-e). On the other hand, SOD overexpression in PSC also significantly lessened the lymph gland dispersal of \( 100\% \) and \( \sim\!50\% \) in \( 10.0\ \mathrm{mM} \) \( (P\!<\!0.005) \) and \( 100.0\ \mathrm{mM} \) \( (P\!<\!0.001) \), respectively, as compared to their respective genetic control, \( P c o l{>}G F P \) (Fig. S4f-j). Overexpression of SOD2 in PSC using \( A n t p{>}G F P \) (another PSC driver) also rescued lamellocyte production and lymph gland dispersal upon benzene exposure (Fig. S4k-t). Altogether, these findings suggest that PSC produces ROS upon benzene exposure, which is responsible for lamellocyte production and premature lymph gland dispersal.
3.6. ROS acts upstream of Toll/NF- \( \mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB} \) to regulate immune response against benzene exposure
We observed that the activation of Toll/NF- \( \mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB} \) activation and elevated ROS levels in the PSC upon benzene exposure is essential for a benzene-induced hematopoietic response. Therefore, testing the interrelationship of Toll/NF- \( \mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB} \) and ROS in the PSC upon benzene exposure was pertinent. In this line, the expression of D4-lacZ in the lymph gland by downregulating Duox in the PSC after \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \) of benzene exposure was
analyzed. We found that downregulation of Duox in the PSC (Pcol>Duox RNAi; D4-lacZ) showed a rescue in the D4-lacZ activation in the PSC and also in the lymph gland lobes upon \( 100.0\,\mathrm{mM} \) benzene exposure \( (P<0.001) \) as compared to its genetic control, \( P c o l{>} ; \) D4-lacZ (Fig. 6a–e). We further knockdown Dif in the PSC \( (P c o l{>}G F P{>}D i f R N A i; \) gstD-lacZ) and observed that blocking Toll/NF- \( \kappa \)B has a limited effect on ROS production upon benzene exposure (Fig. 6f–j). Thus, these results suggest that ROS is upstream of Toll/NF- \( \kappa \)B in the PSC, which successfully responds against benzene-induced immune challenge.
3.7. Toll/NF- \( \kappa \)B activation in the PSC upon benzene exposure is necessary for larval survival
Our findings pointed out the role of ROS-dependent Toll/NF- \( \kappa \)B activation in the PSC as a response to the benzene-induced hematopoietic emergency. The development of myelodysplasia due to chronic benzene exposure is usually life-threatening and shows high mortality [48]. In this regard, we examined the role of Toll/NF- \( \kappa \)B in mounting a hematopoietic response against long-term benzene exposure.
Developmental exposure (from first instar to emergence) of \(100.0\ \mathrm{mM}\) benzene to Drosophila larvae resulted in \(35\%\) mortality \((P\!<\!0.001)\) (Fig. 7). Interestingly, Dif knockdown in the PSC \((P c o l{>}D i f\ N R A i)\) resulted in more larval mortality \(55\%\) ( \(\sim\!57\%\) increased, compared to \(P c o l{>};P\!<\!0.001)\) at \(100.0\;\mathrm{mM}\) benzene exposure compared to untreated control. Further, overexpression of SOD2 \((P c o l{>}S O D2)\) reduced the benzene-induced larval lethality by \(\sim\!30\%\) \(\sim\!15\%\) mortality; \(P{<}0.001)\) . While, blocking Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling in PSC had more deprecative effects on larval survival, alleviating oxidative stress by quenching ROS \((P c o l{>}S O D2)\) improved larval survivability, suggesting the importance of ROS-mediated Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) activation in mounting response against
benzene exposure (Fig. 7).
## 4. Discussion
The ubiquitous nature of benzene makes it inevitable to exposure, especially in occupational setup, and results in several hematological abnormalities like aplastic anemia, myelodysplasia, and bone marrow failure [11,12]. Although several studies elucidated the mechanism of benzene-induced hematopoietic perturbation, the role of the hematopoietic niche or microenvironment is mainly unexplored. This study explored a function of ROS/Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling in the hematopoietic
