niche/PSC of the Drosophila lymph gland essential for mounting an immune response against benzene exposure.
The likelihood of benzene exposure to workers in an occupational setup is relatively high, and long-term exposure to benzene in industries results in impaired blood and immune cell count [49–51]. Our study confirmed that benzene exposure results in irregularities in the lymph gland and immune responses evoked by lamellocyte production. Bone
marrow being the primary target, benzene exposure can severely disrupt immune response either by hindering the differentiation of specific immune cells or detrimental to hematopoietic progenitors [52,53]. Concordant to previous reports, we observed that benzene exposure resulted in a decline in plasmatocytes, the Drosophila macrophage-like cells, and HSC/HPCs due to apoptosis [54–57].
As a normal physiological phenomenon, dispersal of the lymph gland
