is observed during the transition from the late 3rd instar larva to pupa. However, this premature bursting of the lymph gland upon benzene exposure \((96\,\mathrm{h})\) suggests the response shown by the immune system to chemical-induced stress. It is rather astonishing how benzene exposure induced emergency hematopoiesis activation, a similar befitting immune response to stress conditions against wasp and microbial infection, as shown in previous studies [43,58,59].
The bone marrow microenvironment or niche plays a vital role in blood cell differentiation and self-renewal of the stem cell pool [60–62]. Previous studies have shown that benzene and its metabolites negatively affected the hematopoietic microenvironment by interfering in cellular communications by damaging gap junctions and blocking angiogenesis [63,64], however, our study did not observe significant changes in niche cell number or its morphology. It has been well established in Drosophila that the PSC of the lymph gland regulates progenitor differentiation and maintenance in a non-cell autonomous manner [43,65–67]. In this line, upon genetic ablation of PSC \((P c o l{>}G F P{>}r p r)\) , we observed a functional role of PSC in the lamellocytes differentiation and premature lymph gland dispersal upon benzene exposure. This data followed the aforementioned studies, demonstrating the role of the hematopoietic niche or PSC in secreting signaling molecules that regulate blood cell differentiation and progenitor maintenance in normal and immune-challenged conditions [43,58,68,69].
In Drosophila, the production of lamellocytes is mainly regulated by a few signaling molecules based on various stress conditions like Toll/NF\(\mathbf{\kappa}\mathbf{B}\) signaling upon wasp infestation; JAK/STAT upon mechanical stress, and loss of Notch in the PSC [43,67,70]. This study observed robust Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) activation in the PSC and expansion towards progenitors. Our findings agree with a previous study, which by using mRNA Seq analysis, showed that NFKB, a gene that plays a major role in the immune and inflammatory response, was most commonly activated in all groups of workers who were occupationally exposed to benzene [71]. Previously, stimulation with lipopolysaccharide (LPS) activated the Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) axis that drove Myd88 mediated differentiation of myeloid cells [21,72]. This is in corroboration with our findings, where RNAi-mediated knockdown of Dif, Myd88, and overexpression of Toll in the hematopoietic niche further confirmed the role of Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling pathway in lamellocyte production and the lymph gland dispersal upon benzene exposure.
ROS have efficiently marked their role in developing and progressing hematopoietic disorders. Benzene and its metabolites have been well established as the chemical stressor for their ability to induce oxidative stress and damage cellular macromolecules [55,73,74]. We observed higher ROS levels in both HSC/HPCs and PSC upon benzene exposure, in accordance with previous studies that established benzene-induced oxidative stress's role in genotoxicity and bone marrow failure [75, 76]. Duox is a known producer of ROS, and its role in xenobiotic-induced oxidative stress has been well documented [77–80]. In corroboration with the aforementioned studies, we observed that Duox, from the PSC regulates the benzene-induced ROS generation in the lymph gland, resulting in lamellocytes production and lymph gland dispersal. The interplay of Duox and Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) is debatable. Various findings have suggested that Toll-like receptors regulate Nox/Duox mediated ROS production [81–83]. In contrast, our results indicate that activation of Duox might be a prerequisite for ROS production in the PSC and the lymph gland. Further, this ROS activates the Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling in the PSC and the subsequent relay events of lamellocyte production and lymph gland dispersal. These findings were concordant with other studies, where Orosz et al. [79] observed activation of Toll signaling by Duox-induced ROS production upon exposure to cigarette smoke. Furthermore, ROS by Duox2 is important for activating TLR5-mediated inflammatory response in airway mucosa [84]. In mammals, activations of Toll-like receptors (TLRs) in bone marrow niche were observed in a phenomenon of 'emergency granulopoiesis', a condition that produces neutrophils via de novo pathway upon bacterial infections [58,59]. A similar condition called emergency hematopoiesis was observed in the Drosophila lymph gland, where the hematopoietic niche regulated the immune response by activating Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling upon wasp infestation [43]. Intriguingly, this study establishes a role of the niche (PSC) in structuring immune response by activating Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signalling against benzene-induced hematopoietic emergency.
Hematopoietic adversities caused by long-term exposure to benzene include grievous and life-threatening symptoms. A cohort study by Li et al. [48] outlines the relationship between exposure to volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and mortality. The study reported an increase in mortality rate due to myelodysplastic syndrome caused by VOCs like benzene, toluene, xylene, and many others. Concordant to this study, we observed mortality upon developmental benzene exposure. Further, knockdown of Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) axis aggravated the mortality rate, suggesting the importance of Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling in a benzene-induced hematopoietic emergency. Although NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling activates inflammation in the tissue, its pro-survival role has been less admired. During stress, ROS-mediated activation of NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) results in the inhibition of JNK-mediated apoptosis, thus proving its worth in cellular recovery under stress conditions [85,86].
## 5. Conclusion
The hematopoietic organ's key role is to produce substantial blood cells and ensure homeostasis. However, due to extensive activity, bone marrow, as a whole, is under constant threat from various damaging agents, especially immunosuppressive chemicals. In this study, using Drosophila hematopoietic organ, the lymph gland we showed that exposure to benzene adversely affects the process of hematopoiesis. However, the immune system has its way of responding to these chemical-induced threats. We observed that hematopoietic niche or PSC was crucial in mounting a hematopoietic response against benzene. Our findings showed that oxidative stress due to high levels of ROS was sensed by niche cells and activated Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling in niche cells, and spreading it to HSC/HPCs led to the differentiation of lamellocytes. Activation of Toll/NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{KB}\) signaling also triggered the dispersal of lymph glands resulting in the release of lamellocytes into circulation as part of emergency hematopoiesis, which is probably essential for larval survival. However, this conclusion needs further investigation. Overall, our
findings show that the hematopoietic microenvironment/niche plays a crucial role in responding to benzene-induced hematotoxicity by triggering emergency hematopoiesis.
